RK3588S Orange Pi 5B VOP2 support for pixel clocks above 600 MHz.

rk3588_calc_cru_cfg() drives HDMI with dclk equal to the pixel clock, halving it
only for YUV420. 4K120 needs 1188 MHz, well past the 600 MHz the video port can
run at, so the mode is rejected and 4K120 is only reachable with chroma
subsampling.

The hardware sends two pixels per clock instead. Measured on this board running
Rockchip's own kernel at 3840x2160p120 over FRL5:

  dclk_vop0          594 MHz   = v_pixclk / 2, parented to the system CRU
  hdmi_edp0_pixclk   594 MHz   = dclk / 1
  hdmi_edp0_dclk     297 MHz   = dclk / 2
  dclk_core0         297 MHz   = dclk / 2
  clk_hdmiphy_pixel0   4 GHz   = FRL link rate / 10

Expressed against the pixel clock those interface rates are v_pixclk/2 and
v_pixclk/4, exactly what the sub-600 MHz path already produces, so only dclk and
the dividers hanging off it change.

Also here, because the same function is involved:

  - The divider outputs were passed to ilog2() without every branch assigning
    them. With CONFIG_INIT_STACK_ALL_ZERO that made ilog2(0), and an all-ones
    DCLK_OUT_DIV field was written for every HDMI, eDP and DPI modeset. They now
    start at the identity divider.
  - vop2_crtc_mode_valid() only checked horizontal size, so a mode past the
    datapath limit reached clk_set_rate() instead of being filtered.
  - On an FRL link the HDMI PHY PLL runs at the link rate rather than a multiple
    of the pixel clock, so it must not be selected as the dclk parent. The HDMI
    encoder now records the link type in the CRTC state for that.

